# Third-party notices

Macomprendo's own source code is MIT licensed — see [LICENSE](LICENSE).

**The distributed application is not.** It bundles `SherpaOnnxC.framework`, which has
**espeak-ng statically linked into it**, and espeak-ng is licensed under **GPL-3.0**. The
terms of that licence extend to the combined work, so the `.app` you download from the
Releases page is governed by GPL-3.0, not by MIT alone.

This is disclosure, not a change of intent. The corresponding source for the whole combined
work is public:

- this application — <https://github.com/DZamataev/macomprendo>
- the bundled framework — <https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx>
- espeak-ng itself — <https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng>

espeak-ng is the grapheme-to-phoneme engine behind Local TTS. It is reached through
[piper-phonemize](https://github.com/rhasspy/piper-phonemize), which is itself published as
GPL-3.0-or-later for the same reason, and every downloaded Piper voice ships its own
`espeak-ng-data` directory.

Choosing a different voice does not change this: VITS, Kokoro, Matcha and Kitten all live in
the same framework, and the obligation attaches to distributing the linked binary rather than
to whether espeak runs.

Upstream is removing the dependency —
[k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx#3731](https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx/issues/3731), *"Breaking
Change: Remove espeak-ng and piper-phonemize"* — because it is incompatible with
sherpa-onnx's own Apache-2.0 licence. That issue is open, not merged, and targets a breaking
2.0.0 release. When it lands and this app adopts it, the notice above stops applying.

